module gunbc.required_regen_obligation_disposition

import std.types { Int, String, Bool }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }

// WAVE 4 LACKS A TOTAL DISPOSITION FOR THE REQUIRED-REGEN OBLIGATION: TRANSFER AND RETIREMENT
// ARE FUSED.
//
// `gunbc.plans.cli_run_hollowing_plan` carries one row -- area "Regen oracle & self-host scope",
// authority `v2.workflow.required_regen` / `v1_compiler.required_regen_host` -- dispositioned
// `delete-with-v1` at the Wave 4 real-fixpoint cutover. That row answers for two futures with
// different obligations: TRANSFER, where the operation survives under a successor and owes an
// equivalence receipt, and RETIRE, where the operation ceases and owes proof its subject is empty.
// Transfer itself splits again by whether the successor replaces the REALIZATION or the AUTHORITY.
// Both end in deleting the same two files, which is why one row can cover them and why nothing
// notices that it does. `delete-with-v1` states the ACTION and leaves the JUSTIFICATION unstated.
//
// The plan may still choose retirement. What it may not do is let `delete-with-v1` stand in for
// PROVING the subject retired.
//
// DESIGN owns safety standing and the plan owns sequencing; this lifecycle is where they meet, so
// neither is edited against the other. An earlier revision reported the two as CONTRADICTORY and
// asked for arbitration. That was wrong: a next-rung trigger is an obligation WHILE ITS SUBJECT
// REMAINS LIVE, and a terminal migration may remove the subject entirely -- deleting an operation
// whose subject is gone is RETIRING it, not declining to climb. The two are compatible under
// exactly that condition, and nobody had checked whether it holds. That unchecked condition, not
// a contradiction, is the defect.
//
// THIS CARRIER GATES NOTHING, and says so because a carrier named for a wall while occupying none
// is the inflation DESIGN section 4b forbids. It is a finding, not a repair and not a proposal for
// the Wave 4 work.
//
// SCOPE: one obligation, one authority pair, one plan row. How many other plan rows fuse transfer
// with retirement is UNMEASURED, deliberately -- a specimen count taken while establishing the
// mechanism would be mistaken for the bound. What licenses generalizing the MECHANISM and not the
// RATE is that nothing in the corpus can refuse over a plan row's disposition: it has no
// executable dependents, so the fail-closed census that normally surfaces a load-bearing gap is
// structurally silent for this class, and the only thing that finds one is somebody attempting
// the work it governs.

// A RETIREMENT'S OUTSTANDING WORK, ENUMERATED RATHER THAN CHECKED. A prior revision carried the
// Retired arm as a LIST of discharged obligations plus a predicate asserting both were present,
// which review 57020 correctly named as predicate dissolution -- two folds each matching a closed
// two-variant coproduct and answering true for one arm and false for the other. The repair is not
// to respell that predicate. It is to notice that the predicate existed only because the carrier
// could represent a retirement that was not retired, and to remove that representation: an
// incompletely discharged retirement is now a DIFFERENT ARM, not a Retired value carrying a short
// list. The invalid state stops being writable, so there is nothing left to validate and the
// predicate dissolves rather than being reformulated (SS5 construction over validation, SS4b
// dissolution on climb -- the production check goes, the witnesses stay).
//
// The list also admitted a state with no meaning: `Retired { discharged: [] }` was representable
// and was neither retired nor prepared. Enumerating the incomplete states removes that too.
type RetirementProgress
  = MirrorPopulationStillOccupied
  | RequiredConsumersRemain
  | BothObligationsOutstanding

// TRANSFER SPLITS IN TWO, and fusing them was this carrier's own instance of the defect it exists
// to name. A single Transferred arm carrying a successor AUTHORITY described a full authority
// transfer, while the deletion rule below denied deleting the old carrier under it -- so the
// carrier encoded a terminal state in which two definitions answer for one operation, which is
// exactly what SS3 forbids. Which realization performs the work and which authority DEFINES it are
// separate questions with separate deletion consequences, so they are separate arms.
//
// RETIREMENT SPLITS THE SAME WAY AND FOR THE SAME REASON: Retired carries NOTHING, because it
// means both populations are empty and a value that has to be inspected to find out whether it
// means that is not a terminal state.
// ABSORPTION IS A THIRD CONTINUATION AND IT WAS FUSED INTO AuthorityTransferred, which is this
// carrier committing its own subject's defect one level in. An obligation can survive while
// ceasing to be an INDEPENDENTLY NAMED operation -- performed as one inseparable part of a broader
// transaction. That is not an authority transfer: a transfer hands one operation to a successor
// that still answers for it by name, while absorption dissolves the name and owes a COVERAGE
// receipt instead of an equivalence one -- every old input population, ordering guarantee, refusal
// and consumer obligation must map to a named arm of the broader operation.
//
// WHY THE COMPLETED ARM ALONE WOULD HAVE BEEN A DECORATION, stated because the first draft of this
// split was exactly that. A completed absorption has the SAME deletion truth table as an authority
// transfer -- the absorber is canonical, so both files go -- so an Absorbed arm on its own changes
// no predicate outcome and no witness over it can go red for the right reason. It would be a
// richer name over an unchanged wall, which SS4b calls out directly.
//
// THE INCOMPLETE STATE IS WHAT MAKES IT A WALL, and it is the same move retirement already made
// here: a coverage that has not been demonstrated admits NEITHER deletion, which authority
// transfer does not. That difference is authorable by a fixture and refuses.
//
// AbsorptionGap NAMES ONE UNCOVERED CLASS, NOT THE SET, deliberately. A `List<AbsorptionGap>`
// would readmit the empty-list state review 57020 removed from retirement -- a prepared absorption
// carrying no gap is neither prepared nor absorbed. The decision both predicates make turns on
// EXISTENCE of a gap, never on which ones or how many, so a single witness carries everything read
// and nothing that can rot.
type AbsorptionGap
  = InputPopulationUncovered
  | OrderingGuaranteeUncovered
  | RefusalUncovered
  | ConsumerObligationUncovered

// INCOMPLETENESS ITSELF SPLITS, AND COLLAPSING IT WOULD BE NOT-APPLICABLE RENDERED AS MALFORMED --
// DESIGN's most-repeated conflation form, one reason symbol over "no evidence yet" and "the
// evidence says no", which have OPPOSITE OWNERS AND OPPOSITE REPAIRS. Nobody has produced the
// coverage receipt is an ABSENT OBSERVATION: the absorber may cover everything, the remedy is to
// produce the receipt, and it is owned by whoever is landing the absorption. A receipt that SHOWS a
// gap is an observation with a negative result: the remedy is to widen the absorber or refuse the
// absorption, it is owned by whoever owns the broader operation, and the design is wrong.
//
// THE TWO AGREE ON BOTH DELETION PREDICATES, which is exactly why collapsing them is tempting and
// exactly why it is the trap -- a truth-table test cannot see this split, and a carrier whose whole
// subject is a fused disposition must not fuse one two levels in.
//
// THE PRECEDENT IS IN THIS FILE, TWICE, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS NOT SYMMETRY-SEEKING: `RouteStatus`
// separates `RouteRefuted` from `RouteUnexamined`, and `RealizationConformance` separates an
// unobserved conformance from an observed one. Both encode absent-observation against
// negative-result. Collapsing absorption's would leave this module using one vocabulary for that
// distinction in three places and a different one in the fourth.
//
// NOT AN ARM: "absorber not yet identified". An obligation with no named absorber is not being
// absorbed, it is Active, and the carrier already has somewhere to put that.
type AbsorptionCoverage
  = CoverageReceiptUnproduced
  | CoverageReceiptShowsGap { uncovered: AbsorptionGap }

type ObligationLifecycle
  = Active
  | RealizationTransferred { successor_realization: DeclarationRef }
  | AuthorityTransferred {
      successor_authority: DeclarationRef
      successor_realization: DeclarationRef
    }
  | AbsorptionPrepared { coverage: AbsorptionCoverage }
  | AbsorbedIntoBroaderOperation { absorber: DeclarationRef }
  | RetirementPrepared { outstanding: RetirementProgress }
  | Retired

// THE ASYMMETRY IS THE ENTIRE CONTENT, and a symmetric pair would be the fused row again in typed
// clothing. The HOST is one realization: either transfer moves its work to a successor and a
// retirement ends it, so those arms admit deleting it. The CARRIER is the operation's DEFINITION,
// and the two transfers part company there. Under a REALIZATION transfer the carrier stays
// canonical -- the successor realization consumes it -- so deleting it would destroy the referent
// of the equivalence receipt that transfer owes. Under an AUTHORITY transfer the successor
// authority becomes canonical, so the old carrier MUST delete or the operation has two
// definitions; the continuation evidence has to live outside it by then.
//
// A PREPARED RETIREMENT ADMITS NEITHER, and it is the only non-Active arm that does not: no
// terminal deletion is authorized from a prepared state, because the thing that would justify it
// has not happened yet.
fn host_deletion_admitted(lifecycle: ObligationLifecycle) -> Bool {
  match lifecycle {
    Active => false
    RealizationTransferred { successor_realization: _ } => true
    AuthorityTransferred { successor_authority: _, successor_realization: _ } => true
    AbsorptionPrepared { coverage: _ } => false
    AbsorbedIntoBroaderOperation { absorber: _ } => true
    RetirementPrepared { outstanding: _ } => false
    Retired => true
  }
}

fn carrier_deletion_admitted(lifecycle: ObligationLifecycle) -> Bool {
  match lifecycle {
    Active => false
    RealizationTransferred { successor_realization: _ } => false
    AuthorityTransferred { successor_authority: _, successor_realization: _ } => true
    AbsorptionPrepared { coverage: _ } => false
    AbsorbedIntoBroaderOperation { absorber: _ } => true
    RetirementPrepared { outstanding: _ } => false
    Retired => true
  }
}

type RouteStatus
  = RouteRefuted
  | RouteUnexamined

type DerivationRoute {
  route: String
  status: RouteStatus
}

// REFUTED, and the three preconditions below are its evidence. `regen_source_roots()` is a closed
// two-variant enum -- src/v1 and dag -- so src/v2 is not a regen root; measured by reading line 2
// of every emitted stage0 .rs, ZERO declare a v2.* source module. Relocating the carrier into dag/
// does not suffice, because closure membership is by IMPORT FROM A SEED MODULE rather than by
// residence. And no seed-side consumer of regen refusals exists, because the adjudication is
// hand-written Rust; the only honest candidate consumer is the host itself.
// `dag/std/trait_derive_shape.dag` states the exclusion is deliberate: stage0 IS the v1 seed, and
// a seed reaching into src/v2 would depend on the successor it bootstraps toward.
data route_seed_import_generated_type: DerivationRoute = DerivationRoute {
  route: "host imports a Rust type generated into the v1 stage0 closure",
  status: RouteRefuted
}

// Does not require the carrier to be a closure member at all -- the generation step runs before or
// beside the seed build rather than inside it, so the src/v2 root exclusion does not reach it.
data route_build_time_generation: DerivationRoute = DerivationRoute {
  route: "generate the host decision fold from the carrier at build time",
  status: RouteUnexamined
}

// The interpreter already executes .dag; the host would consume a receipt rather than a generated
// type, so no Rust type needs to exist in the seed closure.
data route_interpreted_receipt: DerivationRoute = DerivationRoute {
  route: "interpret the carrier and pass a typed receipt across the host boundary",
  status: RouteUnexamined
}

// Dissolves the boundary instead of crossing it: if the operation runs in the v2 product there is
// no v1 host to derive. This is the TRANSFER arm of the lifecycle above.
data route_promoted_v2_product: DerivationRoute = DerivationRoute {
  route: "move the operation to the promoted v2 workflow product",
  status: RouteUnexamined
}

// If Wave 4 eliminates the committed stage0-mirror population and no successor needs the
// operation, there is nothing left to derive. This is the RETIRED arm, and it is the plan's
// apparent intent -- but it is unproven, which is this module's subject.
data route_retire_with_subject: DerivationRoute = DerivationRoute {
  route: "the operation retires with its subject",
  status: RouteUnexamined
}

// AN EARLIER REVISION CONCLUDED THE CLASS COULD NOT CLIMB, from three refuted attempts at the FIRST
// route only. That is an impossibility claim resting on the exhaustion of one construction, and it
// is the same equivalence-across-a-gap this lane had already caught itself making elsewhere. The
// evidence licenses only that the seed-import route is unavailable.
data required_regen_derivation_routes: List<DerivationRoute> = [
  route_seed_import_generated_type,
  route_build_time_generation,
  route_interpreted_receipt,
  route_promoted_v2_product,
  route_retire_with_subject
]

// DERIVED, NEVER STORED, so a stale verdict is unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged.
fn some_derivation_route_remains_unexamined() -> Bool {
  required_regen_derivation_routes |> any(r => match r.status {
    RouteUnexamined => true
    RouteRefuted => false
  })
}

fn refuted_route_count() -> Int {
  length(xs: required_regen_derivation_routes |> filter(r => match r.status {
    RouteRefuted => true
    RouteUnexamined => false
  }))
}

// THE FIELD CARRIES THE DISTINCTION ITS CONSUMERS READ, AND NOTHING MORE. An earlier revision
// carried seed_importers as an Int, and review 57043 was right that a transcribed integer sat
// oddly beside this module's own retraction of transcribed counts. Measured against the
// consumers rather than argued: every reader tested `== 0` or `> 0`, so the MAGNITUDE was read
// by nothing and a row could drift from one importer to seven with no witness changing. That is
// the same test this file applies to the conformance counts a few declarations up -- a member
// that can rot without changing any decision was never carrying one -- so the honest move is to
// delete the magnitude rather than defend it as a fixture. What remains is the zero/non-zero
// distinction the rule actually turns on, which is a structural fact about the import graph and
// not a measurement of a population. `emitted` stays a stored Bool because it IS read directly:
// it is the observed consequence the control exists to pair against reachability.
type ClosureMembershipObservation {
  module_path: String
  seed_reachable: Bool
  emitted: Bool
}

// BOTH ARMS ARE LOAD-BEARING. A positive-only list would establish that imported modules are
// emitted and leave "would an unimported one be emitted anyway?" unanswered -- which is exactly
// the question the second precondition of the refuted route turns on.
data required_regen_closure_membership_controls: List<ClosureMembershipObservation> = [
  ClosureMembershipObservation { module_path: "extdeps.cargo", seed_reachable: true, emitted: true },
  ClosureMembershipObservation { module_path: "gunbc.cli_dispatch_surface", seed_reachable: true, emitted: true },
  ClosureMembershipObservation { module_path: "gunbc.ci_spec", seed_reachable: false, emitted: false },
  ClosureMembershipObservation { module_path: "gunbc.commit_workflow", seed_reachable: false, emitted: false },
  ClosureMembershipObservation { module_path: "gunbc.doc_graph_roots", seed_reachable: false, emitted: false }
]

// THE OPERATION IS ENFORCED; WHAT IS UNOBSERVED IS THE RELATION BETWEEN ITS TWO REALIZATIONS.
// Across all of src/v1/stage0/src/*.rs the carrier's operation symbols occur only inside a COMMENT
// naming the carrier as authority for an ordering. No production Rust calls into the carrier; its
// only executor is its own witness test.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION READ THAT AS THE OPERATION BEING UNENFORCED. It is not, and the correction
// changes both severity and repair: the hand-written host is an INDEPENDENT REALIZATION and it
// does enforce required-regen -- it refuses drift, names paths, stops the line. What is missing is
// a mechanically enforced RELATION between the two realizations, not the enforcement.
//
// A PRIOR REVISION CARRIED THAT RELATION AS FIVE TRANSCRIBED COUNTS, and review 57020 was right to
// refuse them: they were measured off a live-tree scan with no instrument named, which is exactly
// what the 2026-08-24 ruling forbids -- a transcribed number is unreachable from the thing that
// owns it, so it rots without anyone touching either end. The repair is not to re-source them but
// to observe that they were never load-bearing. The standing this carrier needs is QUALITATIVE:
// whether a conformance relation has been observed at all. The magnitude of the divergence changes
// that answer not at all, and the predicate that consumed the counts read `false` regardless of
// whether they were current -- so the counts could rot silently while the decision stayed right,
// which is the definition of a member carrying no decision.
//
// CONFORMANCE IS A SEPARATE AXIS FROM LIFECYCLE, not an arm of it. The operation's fate and the
// state of the relation between its realizations are orthogonal: the operation is Active today AND
// its realization conformance is unobserved, and fusing them would make one row answer for two
// independent questions.
type RealizationConformance
  = IndependentRealizationConformanceUnobserved
  | RealizationConformanceObserved { receipt: DeclarationRef }
  | NoIndependentRealizationExists

// The observed standing, and it is an assertion of ABSENCE rather than a measurement -- which is
// why it needs no instrument. Naming an instrument is owed by a number that claims to describe the
// tree; this claims only that nothing in the tree relates the two realizations, and the arm that
// would displace it (RealizationConformanceObserved) is uninhabitable until a receipt exists to
// fill it.
data required_regen_realization_conformance: RealizationConformance = IndependentRealizationConformanceUnobserved

fn conformance_is_observed(c: RealizationConformance) -> Bool {
  match c {
    IndependentRealizationConformanceUnobserved => false
    RealizationConformanceObserved { receipt: _ } => true
    NoIndependentRealizationExists => false
  }
}

data required_regen_obligation_authorities: List<DeclarationRef> = [
  decl_ref(module_path: "v2.workflow.required_regen", decl_name: "required_regen_run"),
  decl_ref(module_path: "v2.workflow.required_regen", decl_name: "required_regen_sync_admission"),
  decl_ref(module_path: "v2.workflow.required_regen", decl_name: "RequiredRegenRefusal"),
  decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.plans.cli_run_hollowing_plan", decl_name: "cli_run_hollowing_plan_body")
]

// THE SPLIT IS READ, not merely declared. Without a consumer, `AbsorptionCoverage` would be the
// richer name over an unchanged wall that this module objects to elsewhere -- so the distinction
// answers the question whose two states have different owners: is the absorption UNPROVEN, or has
// it been REFUTED? A refuted absorption is a design defect owned by the broader operation; an
// unproven one is an outstanding receipt owned by whoever is landing it. Shaped exactly like
// `some_derivation_route_remains_unexamined` above, and for the same reason.
fn absorption_coverage_is_refuted(coverage: AbsorptionCoverage) -> Bool {
  match coverage {
    CoverageReceiptUnproduced => false
    CoverageReceiptShowsGap { uncovered: _ } => true
  }
}
